{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>You shall surely die.</b> The intent is not that he would die on the day he ate of it, but that he would become subject to death. For according to the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory, had he not sinned he would never have died, but would have existed eternally like the ministering angels." "<b>And according to</b> the opinion of the philosophers, had the man not sinned he would have died a natural death, because every composite thing ultimately separates and dies a natural death, for it is a necessary consequence in the way of nature that every composite returns to its root and its origin. According to this, the intent of the verse 'you shall surely die' is an accidental death such as most people die by; and their opinion is that even had the man not sinned he would have died a natural death, and when he sinned, his sin caused an accidental death to prevail upon him, so that he would die before his time, like one liable to death by the hand of Heaven. And the matter of the doubling of 'death' is because accidental deaths are of two kinds, in the manner of what is written (I Samuel 26:10): ״כי אם ה׳ יגפנו או יומו יבא ומת או במלחמה ירד ונספה״ — 'either HaShem will strike him, or his day will come and he will die, or he will go down into battle and perish'; and what it says 'or his day will come' — that is natural death; and since an accidental death prevailed upon him through his sin, it was necessary to prevent him from accessing the Tree of Life of eternal natural life; therefore the Tree of Life is called a tree that attains to natural life until the time of the separation of his composition arrives, saving him from accidental death. And the expression וחי לעולם — 'and he shall live forever' — is not an expression of eternity but of a fixed term, in the manner of (Exodus 21:6) ועבדו לעולם and (I Samuel 1:22) וישב שם עד עולם. And according to their opinion, if he had been prevented from the Tree of Life at the outset and then sinned by eating from it, the same would apply that he would also be prevented from the Tree of Knowledge, so that an accidental death would not prevail upon him after he had already been saved from it by eating of the Tree of Life. And they have proofs for what they rely upon according to their opinion, and I would have written two or three of them, were it not that their opinion runs counter to the fundamental principle, which is the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory, and it is not fitting for us to believe and to establish principles except from the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory, received from the mouth of the prophets, that had he not sinned he would have lived forever; and thus the verse says (Psalms 82:6): ״אני אמרתי אלהים אתם ובני עליון כלכם״ — 'I said, you are Elohim, and all of you are sons of the Most High.' And it did not say 'like Elohim' and 'like sons of the Most High,' but Elohim in truth and sons of the Most High in truth, and the verses on this subject are many." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the doubling of the expression מות תמות is the way of Scripture, and there are many doublings in Scripture similar to these, concerning which our Rabbis of blessed memory said: the Torah spoke in the language of human beings." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'for in the day that you eat of it you shall surely die' — the day of the Holy One, blessed be He, is a thousand years. And so that the word of the Holy One, blessed be He, should not go forth in vain, He gave him one day of His own and he died on that day, for seventy years were subtracted from it for him." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the doubling of the expression is like the doubling of the death of the sons of Aaron, concerning whom it is written (Leviticus 16:1) ״אחרי מות שני בני אהרן וימותו״, or like the prayer of Moses (Deuteronomy 33:6) ״יחי ראובן ואל ימות״ according to the opinion of the Targum. And this is what you find in Abraham, who was a father to the entire world, for it is written of him ״כי אב המון גוים נתתיך״, and Scripture also called him ״האדם הגדול״, for at first he divided and in the end he joined together. And there is in this another opinion, different from what is said in the Midrash: 'I give him seventy years of my years.' And whoever is discerning will understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {4 "The phrase כי בראשונה קצץ ולבסוף יחד — literally 'for at first he cut/divided and in the end he joined together' — is textually opaque; translated hyper-literally. The referent of 'he' and the specific Kabbalistic allusion are left as the author intended."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:48.436461137Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}